 {'idx': 2, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'Domain (biology) In biological taxonomy, a domain [/də(ʊ)ˈmeɪn/] (Latin: "regio"), also superkingdom or empire, is the highest taxonomic rank of organisms in the three-domain system of taxonomy designed by Carl Woese. According to the Woese system, introduced in 1990, the tree of life consists of three domains: Archaea, Bacteria, and Eukarya. The first two are all prokaryotic microorganisms, or single-celled organisms whose cells have no nucleus. All life that has a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles, and multicellular organisms, is included in the Eukarya. The term "domain" was proposed by Woese et al. (1990) in his three-domain system. This term represents', 'title': 'Domain (biology)'}
