[{'idx': 0, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'Jitter (optics) In optics, jitter is used to refer to motion that has high temporal frequency relative to the integration/exposure time. This may result from vibration in an assembly or from the unstable hand of a photographer. Jitter is typically differentiated from smear, which has a lower frequency relative to the integration time. Whereas smear refers to a relatively constant rate during the integration/exposure time, jitter refers to a relatively sinusoidal motion during the integration/exposure time. The equation for the optical Modulation transfer function associated with jitter is where k is the spatial frequency and formula_2 is the amplitude of', 'title': 'Jitter (optics)'}
