 {'idx': 26, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'East England and Scotland. In addition, Scots (including Ulster Scots) is descended from the Northumbrian dialect, as is modern Northumbrian and other dialects of Northern English. Some Scottish and Northumbrian folk still say or "our father" and "thou art". <poem style="margin-left: 2em;" lang="ang"> FADER USÆR ðu arð in heofnu Sie gehalgad NOMA ÐIN. Tocymeð RÍC ÐIN. Sie WILLO ÐIN suæ is in heofne and in eorðo. HLAF USERNE of\'wistlic sel ús todæg, and f\'gef us SCYLDA USRA, suæ uoe f\'gefon SCYLDGUM USUM. And ne inlæd usih in costunge, ah gefrig usich from yfle. </poem> Northumbrian dialect (Old English) Northumbrian was', 'title': 'Northumbrian dialect (Old English)'}]

