 {'idx': 1, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'Lexington, California Lexington, California, is a ghost town in Santa Clara County, now submerged by the Lexington Reservoir. Originally located along Los Gatos Creek, the town was 550 feet above sea level. Lexington started out as a sawmill built in 1848 by Isaac Branham and Julian Jank. Zachariah "Buffalo" Jones bought the mill for $3000 and laid out a town called "Jones Mill". In 1860 John P. Hennings bought some of the property and changed the name to Lexington, after his home town of Lexington, Kentucky. Lexington was a stop on the stagecoach route from Los Gatos to Santa Cruz.', 'title': 'Lexington, California'}
